          %h2#bar_and_cobar_constructions Bar and cobar constructions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is a brief entry at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+construction"} bar construction
                together with a blog link
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd Trimble
                  ,
                  %em On the Bar Construction
                  (
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/05/on_the_bar_construction.html"}> blog
                  )
            %li
              %p
                There is some discussion of the bar-cobar adjointness as it relates to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisting+cochain"} twisting cochains
            %li
              %p Here we will concentrate on the bar-cobar adjointness itself and start exploring the links with other parts of differential algebra.
          %p
            One of the earliest examples of a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functors
            studied in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            was that giving the relationship between the functors for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspension"} reduced suspension
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} based loop space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+space"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,x_0)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspension"} reduced suspension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma X
            is obtained by taking the cylinder
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\times X
            and identifying the subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi I
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}\times X\cup I\times \{x_0\}
            to a point. (Think of crushing the two ends of the cylinder and the line through the base point to a point.) This can also be thought of as forming
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1\wedge X
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            of the circle with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Adjoint to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loop+space"} based loop space
            functor:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega Y
            is the space of pointed maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∨
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1\to S^1\vee S^1
            as described at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em ‘subdivision as an inverse for composition’
          %p (perhaps: Picture to go here?)
          %p
            Using ordinary (co)homology to study spaces such as CW-complexes, we naturally use the complexes of (cellular) chains on spaces. The structure of chains on the suspension is easy to work out using the obvious cellular structure, but that on the loop space is much harder as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
            is given the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
            and only has the homotopy type of a CW-complex, so no nice cellular structure is given us ‘on a plate’. The idea is thus to start with a chain complex model,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_*(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_*(X)
            a ‘model’ for the chain complex of the loop space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Both directions use an abstract algebraic model of concatenation of paths and so their construction is linked to that of free monoids, and through those to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
            and related abstract machinery to handle concatenation and its higher categorical analogues in categorical contexts.
          %p
            The chain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_*(X)
            has a rich coalgebraic structure induced by a cellular diagonal approximation on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            so the
            %em cobar
            construction will start with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+coalgebra"} dg-coalgebra
            as ‘input’ and as output we will hope for both a coalgebra structure (reflecting the chain coalgebra idea) and an algebra structure (coming from modelling the concatenation of loops). We therefore might hope for, and in fact do get, a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+Hopf+algebra"} differential graded Hopf algebra
          %p
            Going the other way, we start with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            and use ‘coconcatenation’ or ‘subdivision’ to get a coalgebra structure. In fact, once again, this is a Hopf algebra.
          %p These topologically motivated constructions can be applied in much greater generality as we will see both here and elsewhere:
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#the_bar_construction The bar construction
          %p (due originally Eilenberg-MacLane) Remember this goes from ‘algebras’ to Hopf algebras in general.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi pre
                  %mi ε
                  %mi CDGA
                  %mo →
                  %mi pre
                  %mi CDGHA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B :pre \varepsilon CDGA \to pre CDGHA
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ε
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,d,\varepsilon)
            be a commutative, augmented differential
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(A_n)\subseteq A_{n-1}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mi Ker
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{A} = Ker \varepsilon
          %p
            The
            %strong bar construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ε
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(A,d,\varepsilon)
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ε
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(A,d,\varepsilon) = (T(s\overline{A}), D),
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(s\overline{A})
                is the commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+Hopf+algebra"} differential graded Hopf algebra
                generated by
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mover
                          %mi A
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\overline{A}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                being the
                %em suspension
                (shift, translation, etc) operator discussed in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector spaces
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi I
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = d_I + d_E
                , where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi s
